; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Gravity Orbits
; CATEGORY: Animation & Many Turtles
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     physics, Newton, gravity, simulation, velocity, trails
; SUMMARY:  Simulates Newton's law of gravity: planets launched at different speeds trace circles, ellipses and an escaping path.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   A heavy star sits in the middle. Four planet turtles start at the
;   same distance on its left, each launched upward at a different
;   speed. The program does not draw circles on purpose: it only
;   applies Newton's law (the pull gets four times weaker when the
;   distance doubles) over and over. Out come a near circle, two
;   ellipses and one planet so fast it escapes into space.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run and watch the trails. Press Stop to end. Change the
;   speeds in the list :launch.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * position and velocity as pairs of numbers
;   * the inverse square law: pull = strength / distance squared
;   * Euler steps: update velocity from the pull, then position
;   * arrays of positions and velocities, one entry per planet
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Find the speed that gives a perfect circle (about 5).
;   * Launch a planet downward with a negative speed.
;   * Halve :strength. Which planets escape now?
;   * The red orbit slowly turns. Real orbits (almost) do not; that
;     is the error from taking whole steps. Smaller steps shrink it.
; ============================================================

; STEP.PLANET moves planet :k by one small step of time.
to step.planet :k
  localmake "x item :k :xs
  localmake "y item :k :ys
  localmake "r2 :x * :x + :y * :y
  ; Too close to the star? The planet has crashed; leave it alone.
  if :r2 < 400 [stop]
  localmake "r sqrt :r2
  ; The pull points at the star (at 0, 0), so its x and y parts
  ; are -x / r and -y / r, times the size strength / r^2.
  localmake "pull :strength / :r2
  setitem :k :vxs (item :k :vxs) - :pull * :x / :r
  setitem :k :vys (item :k :vys) - :pull * :y / :r
  setitem :k :xs :x + item :k :vxs
  setitem :k :ys :y + item :k :vys
  ; Only move the turtle if the planet is still on screen.
  if and (abs item :k :xs) < 400 (abs item :k :ys) < 300 [
    ask :k [setxy item :k :xs item :k :ys]
  ]
end

; LAUNCH.ALL puts the planets at x = -200 and sets them moving.
to launch.all
  localmake "n count :launch
  make "xs array :n
  make "ys array :n
  make "vxs array :n
  make "vys array :n
  for [k 1 :n] [
    setitem :k :xs -200
    setitem :k :ys 0
    setitem :k :vxs 0
    setitem :k :vys item :k :launch
    ask :k [
      penup setxy -200 0 setshape "circle
      setpencolor item :k :colors setpensize 2
      showturtle pendown
    ]
  ]
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [5 5 20]
; the star
setpencolor [255 210 90] setpensize 40 dot [0 0]
make "strength 5000
make "launch [3 4 5 7.5]
make "colors [[240 90 80] [250 180 60] [90 200 120] [110 160 255]]
launch.all
forever [
  ; several physics steps per frame keep the orbits accurate
  repeat 4 [for [k 1 count :launch] [step.planet :k]]
  wait 1
]
